CAT = "pcocRules"; SEG = "REG"
Q = [
("Who must carry proof of competency on Canadian waters?",
 "Operators of motorized pleasure craft", ["Owners of boats over 6 m long", "Only operators of personal watercraft", "Operators of boats over 40 hp"],
 "Proof of competency is required to operate any recreational boat fitted with a motor, whatever its size or power. Length, engine size and boat type do not change the rule."),
("You fit a small electric trolling motor to your canoe. Do you need proof of competency?",
 "Yes, any motor counts", ["No, electric motors are exempt", "Only above 10 hp", "Only on salt water"],
 "Transport Canada applies the rule to all motor types, electric trolling motors included. A paddled canoe with no motor needs no proof of competency."),
("You are sailing a sailboat that has an outboard motor tilted up and switched off. Do you need proof of competency?",
 "Yes, even with the motor off", ["No, only while the motor runs", "No, sailboats are always exempt", "Only when sailing after sunset"],
 "Proof of competency is needed on a boat with a motor even when the motor is not in use, such as while sailing."),
("How long is a Pleasure Craft Operator Card valid?",
 "For life", ["For 5 years", "For 10 years", "Until age 65"],
 "The Pleasure Craft Operator Card does not expire and never needs renewing. It is a different document from the pleasure craft licence, which belongs to the boat, not the operator."),
("Which of these is accepted as proof of competency on board?",
 "The original operator card", ["A photocopy of the card", "A photo of the card on a phone", "The test results e-mail"],
 "Transport Canada does not accept a paper or electronic copy of a Pleasure Craft Operator Card. Carry the card itself."),
("Without direct supervision, an operator under 12 may drive a boat with a motor of at most:",
 "10 hp (7.5 kW)", ["25 hp (18.6 kW)", "40 hp (30 kW)", "5 hp (3.7 kW)"],
 "An operator under 12 without direct supervision is limited to 10 hp (7.5 kW). The 40 hp limit is for ages 12 to 15."),
("Without direct supervision, an operator aged 12 to 15 may drive a boat with a motor of at most:",
 "40 hp (30 kW)", ["10 hp (7.5 kW)", "60 hp (45 kW)", "25 hp (18.6 kW)"],
 "Operators aged 12 to 15 without direct supervision may use a motor of up to 40 hp (30 kW). Under 12 the limit drops to 10 hp."),
("What is the minimum age to operate a personal watercraft in Canada?",
 "16 years", ["12 years", "14 years", "18 years"],
 "No one under 16 may operate a personal watercraft, with or without supervision."),
("For the youth horsepower limits, direct supervision means:",
 "A person 16 or older in the boat", ["A parent watching from shore", "An adult in another boat nearby", "A person 18 or older on the dock"],
 "Direct supervision means someone aged 16 or older is in the boat and directly supervising the young operator. Watching from shore or another boat does not count."),
("A completed Rental Boat Safety Checklist counts as proof of competency for how long?",
 "Only for the rental period", ["For the whole boating season", "For 12 months from the rental", "For life, like the card"],
 "The rental checklist is accepted only while you have that rental boat. For any other boat you need a card or other accepted proof."),
("A visitor brings their own motorboat into Canada for 30 days. Do they need Canadian proof of competency?",
 "No, under 45 consecutive days", ["Yes, from their first day here", "Only if staying over 14 days", "Only on the Great Lakes"],
 "Proof of competency is not required for a non-resident operating a boat brought into Canada for fewer than 45 consecutive days. Visitors using a Canadian boat can show proof that meets their home rules."),
("A pleasure craft licence is required when the boat's engines total at least:",
 "10 hp (7.5 kW)", ["40 hp (30 kW)", "25 hp (18.6 kW)", "5 hp (3.7 kW)"],
 "Under the Small Vessel Regulations, a pleasure craft powered by one or more engines totalling 7.5 kW (10 hp) or more must be licensed or registered."),
("Where must a pleasure craft licence number be displayed?",
 "On both sides of the bow", ["On the stern, both corners", "On the windshield frame", "On one side of the hull"],
 "The licence number goes on both sides of the bow, as high above the waterline as practical, in block characters that contrast with the hull."),
("The characters of a pleasure craft licence number must be at least how tall?",
 "7.5 cm", ["2.5 cm", "15 cm", "5 cm"],
 "Licence characters must be block letters and numbers at least 7.5 cm high, in a colour that contrasts with the background."),
("What licence document must be on board while you operate a licensed pleasure craft?",
 "A copy of the licence", ["The bill of sale", "The engine's warranty", "The dealer's invoice"],
 "A licensed pleasure craft may only be operated with a copy of its licence on board, and the owner's name and address on it must be up to date."),
("The owner must update a pleasure craft licence when:",
 "Their name or address changes", ["The boat moves to a new lake", "New lifejackets are bought", "A passenger seat is added"],
 "A licensed pleasure craft may be operated only if the owner's name and address on the licence are accurate, so changes must be reported to Transport Canada. Moving the boat or changing equipment does not affect the licence."),
("Who issues Pleasure Craft Operator Cards?",
 "Accredited course providers", ["Provincial licence offices", "Canadian Coast Guard stations", "Local marinas and dealers"],
 "Transport Canada sets the rules and accredits course providers, and the provider whose test you pass issues the card."),
("You lose your operator card. Where do you get a replacement?",
 "From the provider that issued it", ["From any marina office", "From the Canadian Coast Guard", "From a provincial licence office"],
 "Replacement cards come from the course provider that issued the original. Transport Canada has an online lookup to find your provider."),
("Operating a boat while impaired by alcohol or drugs is:",
 "A Criminal Code offence", ["Only a provincial fine", "Legal at anchor offshore", "Legal for passengers to direct"],
 "Impaired operation of a vessel is a Criminal Code offence, just as it is for a car. Provinces also set their own rules on alcohol on board."),
("Who is responsible for damage caused by your boat's wake?",
 "The operator of the boat", ["The owner of the dock", "Nobody, wakes are natural", "The other boat's operator"],
 "You are responsible for your wake and any damage or injury it causes. Slow down near docks, shorelines, swimmers and small boats."),
("What should you do with garbage while boating?",
 "Bring it back to shore", ["Sink it in very deep water", "Burn it on board", "Drop it far offshore"],
 "Throwing garbage overboard is prohibited in Canadian waters. Store it on board and dispose of it properly ashore."),
("Your bilge water has an oily sheen. What should you do?",
 "Use absorbent pads", ["Pump it out while moving", "Add soap and pump it out", "Pump it out after dark"],
 "Discharging oily water is illegal. Use absorbent pads and dispose of them ashore; soap only hides the oil and is also harmful."),
("Before moving your boat to another lake, what should you do to stop invasive species?",
 "Clean, drain and dry it", ["Rinse only the motor", "Add bleach to the bilge", "Leave water in the bilge"],
 "Clean off plants and mud, drain all water from the bilge, live well and motor, and let the boat dry before launching in a new waterway."),
("A compliance notice fixed to a new boat by its builder confirms that the boat:",
 "Met Canadian construction standards", ["Passed a Coast Guard inspection", "Is licensed to its current owner", "May be run by anyone over 16"],
 "The compliance notice is the builder's statement that the boat met the construction requirements in force when it was built. On boats up to 6 m it also gives recommended limits for people, load and engine power. It is not an inspection, a licence or proof of competency."),
("Which vessels must follow the Collision Regulations?",
 "Every vessel", ["Only commercial ships", "Only boats over 12 m", "Only boats with radios"],
 "The Collision Regulations, Canada's version of the international rules of the road, apply to every vessel, from canoes to ships."),
("Where the shoreline speed limit applies, how fast may you go within 30 m of shore?",
 "10 km/h", ["20 km/h", "30 km/h", "5 km/h"],
 "In waters where the shoreline limit applies, the maximum speed within 30 m of shore is 10 km/h, with some exceptions such as towing a skier straight out from shore."),
("Towing a water-skier is not allowed during which hours?",
 "One hour after sunset to sunrise", ["Sunrise to one hour after sunrise", "Sunset to one hour after sunset", "Sunset to one hour before midnight"],
 "Under the Small Vessel Regulations, towing a person on skis, a tube or other device is prohibited from one hour after sunset until sunrise, and in restricted visibility at any time."),
("When towing a water-skier, the boat must carry:",
 "A spotter besides the operator", ["A second tow rope and handle", "A certified ski instructor aboard", "A flag tied to the tow rope"],
 "Someone other than the operator must watch the towed person, and the boat needs a seat for each person being towed in case they must be brought aboard."),
]
